{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"nl -ba /home/muk/projects/Strata/include/strata/core/hit_hook.hpp; nl -ba /home/muk/projects/Strata/include/strata/core/expert_cache.hpp | sed -n '1,280p'; rg -n 'hit_hook|hit\\\\.run|try_hit|HitFn|ExpertHit|hook|residency' /home/muk/projects/Strata/src/core/session.cpp /home/muk/projects/Strata/include/strata/core/session.hpp /home/muk/projects/Strata/include/strata/core/expert_source.hpp\"","aggregated_output":"     1\t// include/strata/core/hit_hook.hpp - R4.2c: the seam between the host loop and the VRAM expert tier.\n     2\t//\n     3\t// **IT IS ITS OWN HEADER BECAUSE BOTH SIDES NEED IT AND NEITHER SHOULD OWN IT.**  `session.hpp` declares the\n     4\t// loop that calls the hook; `expert_source.hpp` declares the implementation that answers it.  Putting the type\n     5\t// in either one makes the other include a header it has no other use for, and the loop knowing what an expert\n     6\t// cache is (or the adapter knowing what a graph is) is exactly the coupling this seam exists to avoid.\n     7\t#pragma once\n     8\t\n     9\t#include <cstdint>\n    10\t\n    11\tnamespace strata::core {\n    12\t\n    13\t/// **TWO PHASES, AND THE SPLIT IS THE WHOLE POINT.**  `Launch` runs BEFORE the CPU pool and `Combine` runs\n    14\t/// AFTER the misses have been copied into `parts`.  That ordering is what lets the GPU's resident experts run\n    15\t/// at the same time as the CPU's misses instead of after them:\n    16\t///\n    17\t///     ring -> hits(Launch)   [GPU: quantize + grouped experts into `hit_out`]\n    18\t///          -> pool()         [CPU: the misses, CONCURRENT with the GPU]\n    19\t///          -> H2D misses into `parts`\n    20\t///          -> hits(Combine)  [GPU: parts += hit_out]\n    21\t///          -> post[l]\n    22\t///\n    23\t/// **MEASURED, AND WHY IT IS NOT THE OBVIOUS ORDER.**  The first version called the hit path once, after the\n    24\t/// pool, writing straight into `parts`.  The drain duly fell **18.2 -> 10.2 ms** - the CPU really was doing\n    25\t/// half the experts - and **the token did not move at all**, because the GPU's half had simply been moved onto\n    26\t/// the critical path behind the CPU's instead of beside it.  A second buffer and one `add_inplace` buy the\n    27\t/// overlap back.\n    28\tenum class HitPhase { Launch, Combine };\n    29\t\n    30\t/// `ids` AND `k` ARE THE ROUTER'S OWN OUTPUT, AND THEY ARE WHY THIS HOOK TAKES ARGUMENTS AT ALL.\n    31\t///\n    32\t/// The first version took only `(user, stream, phase)` and left the hit/miss decision inside the pool\n    33\t/// callback - which runs AFTER `Launch`.  So `Launch` read the hit list from the PREVIOUS layer and computed\n    34\t/// the previous layer's experts into the current layer's rows.  It produced a token, the timings looked\n    35\t/// plausible, and **C1 moved from mean KL 9.69e-02 to 1.03e+00 and top-1 from 0.867 to 0.333.**  The decision\n    36\t/// has to happen in `Launch`, on the ids the doorbell published for THIS layer, and the pool then consumes it.\n    37\t///\n    38\t/// A null `HitFn` means \"no VRAM tier\" - the default, and what every caller had before R4.\n    39\tusing HitFn = void (*)(void* user, void* stream, HitPhase phase, const int32_t* ids, int64_t k);\n    40\t\n    41\t}  // namespace strata::core\n     1\t// include/strata/core/expert_cache.hpp - R4: the VRAM-resident expert tier.\n     2\t//\n     3\t// **WHY THIS EXISTS, IN ONE PARAGRAPH.** The CPU expert pool is the engine's largest single cost: measured,\n     4\t// **663.6 MB of expert bytes per token at ~40 GB/s = 16.2 ms**, on a token of ~53 ms, and `test_expert_pool`\n     5\t// says the pipeline hides **1.055 ms of 19.035** - the CPU work is 96% exposed because the residual chain is\n     6\t// strictly serial. The only way past it is to stop reading those bytes: put some of the 48 x 512 experts in\n     7\t// VRAM and let the GPU compute them, so the CPU reads only the misses.\n     8\t//\n     9\t// **THE DESIGN IS SIZED AGAINST MEASUREMENTS, NOT AGAINST THE PLAN'S ASSUMPTIONS.**\n    10\t//\n    11\t//   * `h_expert` at 4,105 slots, **ten-fold leave-one-out: 0.6447 [0.6110, 0.6750]** (`tools/routing_kfold.py`).\n    12\t//     The 0.6573 the README published is in-sample and 0.4720 measured a single-prompt CORPUS, not the metric.\n    13\t//   * VRAM, measured on a live run rather than at startup: **6414 MiB free** with the engine at steady state,\n    14\t//     against **5413 MiB** for 4,105 slots. It fits, with ~1 GB spare. `strata-device`'s planner agrees and says\n    15\t//     FITS, but the planner runs before the dense arena exists, so the live figure is the one that counts.\n    16\t//   * `h_layer` held-out is **0.0456** - only 4.6% of (layer, token) pairs have all ten experts resident - so\n    17\t//     this cannot be a per-layer grouped kernel and the three-way split is not an optimisation.\n    18\t//\n    19\t// **WHAT THIS FILE IS.** The SLOT STORAGE and the RESIDENCY TABLE: it allocates the VRAM, fills it from the host\n    20\t// arena, and answers `(layer, expert) -> slot or -1`. It computes nothing itself: the GPU computes the experts it\n    21\t// holds in the verify window's grouped kernels and the prompt path (the hit/miss split), and the CPU pool the rest.\n    22\t// (Written when nothing consumed the slots yet and `--expert-cache` defaulted to 0; setup's configs use `auto`.)\n    23\t#pragma once\n    24\t\n    25\t#include <cstddef>\n    26\t#include <cstdint>\n    27\t#include <memory>\n    28\t#include <string>\n    29\t#include <vector>\n    30\t\n    31\t#include \"strata/core/vmm.hpp\"\n    32\t\n    33\tnamespace strata::core {\n    34\t\n    35\t/// Where one expert's VRAM lives, or -1.  `int32_t` and not a bool because the slot index is what the kernel\n    36\t/// needs, and a table that has to be re-derived from a bitmask is a table that will be re-derived differently\n    37\t/// in the two places that use it.\n    38\tinline constexpr int32_t kNotResident = -1;\n    39\t\n    40\t/// **THE STATIC RESIDENCY PLAN, READ BACK FROM THE FILE `tools/make_profile.py` WROTE.**\n    41\t///\n    42\t/// `profile.bin` is `STRP`, then `version, n_layers, n_expert, slots, n_ranked`, then `n_ranked` `(layer,\n    43\t/// expert)` pairs ranked by descending routing frequency, then a `n_layers x n_expert` frequency table.\n    44\t///\n    45\t/// **WHY A PROFILE AND NOT THE COMPULSORY-MISS POLICY THIS ENGINE ALREADY HAD.**  Compulsory-miss fills with\n    46\t/// the first `S` DISTINCT experts the run happens to route, which on this workload is whatever the prompt\n    47\t/// touched first - it measured `h = 0.4864`.  A profile ranked by frequency over a whole trace is what the\n    48\t/// plan's `0.6447` refers to, and the CPU's half of the drain is linear in `h`, so the gap is worth about\n    49\t/// 4 ms of drain.  The point of reading it from a file is that a profile built on ONE prompt can be scored on\n    50\t/// ANOTHER, which is the only way to know whether it transfers.\n    51\t///\n    52\t/// The frequency table is not returned: it is the profile's own working, and the engine measures `h` by\n    53\t/// running rather than by re-deriving what the file claims.\n    54\tbool read_expert_profile(const std::string& path, int64_t n_layers, int64_t n_expert,\n    55\t                         std::vector<std::pair<int32_t, int32_t>>& ranked, int64_t& slots, std::string& err);\n    56\t\n    57\t/// #477 (--expert-profile-save): what the adaptive tier learned, as a profile ranking EVERY (layer, expert) pair -\n    58\t/// the experts resident in VRAM now first (where the swaps left the cache), then the rest; within each, by `heat`\n    59\t/// (the routing the adaptive tier counted since the start, descending), then by `prior` (the profile the engine\n    60\t/// started from: an expert this run never routed keeps its old place), then by index.  A start from it begins\n    61\t/// where this one ended; one with more slots adds the hottest of the rest, one with fewer keeps the hottest.\n    62\t/// `resident` and `heat`: n_layers x n_expert entries (`heat` may be empty: no counts, the prior decides).\n    63\tstd::vector<std::pair<int32_t, int32_t>> rank_learned_profile(int64_t n_layers, int64_t n_expert,\n    64\t                                                              const std::vector<uint8_t>& resident,\n    65\t                                                              const std::vector<double>& heat,\n    66\t                                                              const std::vector<std::pair<int32_t, int32_t>>& prior);\n    67\t\n    68\t/// #477: writes `ranked` in tools/make_profile.py's format, byte for byte: `STRP`, `1, n_layers, n_expert,\n    69\t/// n_ranked, n_ranked` (uint32), the pairs (uint16 layer, uint16 expert), then the n_layers x n_expert int32 table\n    70\t/// of each pair's rank (-1: not ranked).  Atomically: a `<path>.tmp` beside it, renamed over `path` once complete,\n    71\t/// so a reader (the next start) never sees half a file.  False with `err` when it could not be written.\n    72\tbool write_expert_profile(const std::string& path, int64_t n_layers, int64_t n_expert,\n    73\t                          const std::vector<std::pair<int32_t, int32_t>>& ranked, std::string& err);\n    74\t\n    75\t/// Free device memory for the expert cache.  On a unified memory system - one memory for the CPU and the GPU, such as\n    76\t/// the DGX Spark (GB10); CUDA reports it as cudaDevAttrIntegrated - cudaMemGetInfo counts only MemFree, not the page cache - which, with the experts read from the GGUF in place, is mostly those\n    77\t/// files' clean pages and gives way to a device allocation.  There the figure is MemAvailable less 6 GiB for the OS\n    78\t/// and the engine's host side (STRATA_UMA_HEADROOM_GIB).  Elsewhere it is cudaMemGetInfo's.  On Windows with an NVIDIA\n    79\t/// card STRATA_VRAM_FREE=device (opt-in) takes the smaller of that and NVML's figure for the whole card: under WDDM\n    80\t/// cudaMemGetInfo does not count what other processes hold.  It can only make the figure smaller.\n    81\tsize_t device_free_bytes();\n    82\t\n    83\t/// One piece of an expert blob that lives contiguously in a source's own memory: `n` bytes at `p`, to land `at` bytes into the blob.\n    84\tstruct BlobPart { const uint8_t* p; uint64_t n; uint64_t at; };\n    85\t\n    86\tclass ExpertCache {\n    87\tpublic:\n    88\t    ExpertCache() = default;\n    89\t    ~ExpertCache();\n    90\t    ExpertCache(const ExpertCache&) = delete;\n    91\t    ExpertCache& operator=(const ExpertCache&) = delete;\n    92\t\n    93\t    /// Allocates `n_slots * blob_bytes` of DEVICE memory and a `n_layers * n_expert` residency table, and\n    94\t    /// **CHECKS THE ALLOCATION AGAINST WHAT THE CARD ACTUALLY HAS** rather than assuming the planner was right.\n    95\t    /// A cache that silently allocates less than it was asked for would report a hit rate for slots it does\n    96\t    /// not have, which is the shape of error this project keeps paying for.\n    97\t    bool open(int64_t n_slots, int64_t n_layers, int64_t n_expert, int64_t blob_bytes, std::string& err);\n    98\t    /// Plan v0.3 P6: slots of the given sizes, back to back (a native pack's blobs differ per layer, and a\n    99\t    /// profile-filled tier never moves an expert to another layer's slot, so each slot keeps its first size).\n   100\t    bool open_sized(const std::vector<int64_t>& slot_bytes, int64_t n_layers, int64_t n_expert, std::string& err);\n   101\t    /// Byte offset of each slot in the arena (null for uniform slots).\n   102\t    const uint64_t* slot_offsets() const { return off_.empty() ? nullptr : off_.data(); }\n   103\t    void close();\n   104\t\n   105\t    bool valid() const { return base_ != nullptr;