 Graph capture and sync | Cache host sync is outside GPU split graphs; fallback matmul can disallow capture. Eval callback further splits/synchronizes execution. `L:src/llama-moe-cache.cpp:548-550`; `L:ggml/src/ggml-cuda/ggml-cuda.cu:2011-2039,2711-2715`; `L:ggml/src/ggml-backend.cpp:1905-1934`. | Captured pre/post graphs surround host dispatch; stable buffers and current-token staging are explicit. `S:src/core/session.cpp:599-647,670-677,748-784`. | **Yes** if adding mixed dispatch to captured graphs: stable addresses, separate graph phases and carefully scoped host work. | Capture/replay across many positions; no-allocation steady-state check; compare capture-off numerical output and latency. |\n| Prefill versus decode policy | Persistent lookup bypassed for T>32 or insufficient worst-case slots; large batches may read cache without LRU churn. `L:src/llama-moe-cache.cpp:462-512`. | Prefill can borrow expert slots and then refill them; not the same path as hit-hook decode. `S:src/program/generate.cpp:19248-19394`. | **Yes** explicit phase policy, borrow/refill lifecycle if required. **No** need to build borrowing for a first fixed-cache experiment. | Separate prefill/decode traces and times; repeat prefill then decode to check residency retention/refill integrity. |\n| KV/expert capacity | Fit estimates model/context/compute; optional cache has separate accounted memory. No lending or live resize in inspected llama cache class. `L:common/fit.cpp:30-76`; `L:src/llama-context.cpp:3710-3713`; `L:src/llama-moe-cache.cpp:628-638`. | Expert cache exposes segmented shrink/grow and VMM/slot borrowing facilities; caller evicts tail before shrink. `S:include/strata/core/expert_cache.hpp:119-148,230-238`. | **Yes** resource broker/live-resize controls for full reproduction. **No** for initial fixed-budget residency baseline. | Capacity accounting after warmup, reserve margins, resize under load, stale-pointer/replay checks, allocation-pressure recovery. |\n| KV/session reset | Memory clear/remove and state save/load operate memory module, not expert LRU. `L:src/llama-context.cpp:3608-3667,4385-4402`; `L:src/llama-kv-cache.cpp:391-401`. | Slot storage, profile and adaptive residency are separately managed from token state in the studied interfaces. `S:include/strata/core/expert_cache.hpp:86-166`; `S:src/program/generate.cpp:19839-19935`. | **Yes** experimental lifecycle controller distinguishing KV reset, expert cold/warm state, adaptive history and OS cache state. | Repeated identical prompt with new context versus KV-only clear; record hit counters and token positions; session restore should not invent routing for skipped tokens. |\n| Built-in measurements | Bench/perplexity/eval callbacks/scheduler debug/cache aggregates are useful building blocks, not a single residency oracle. `L:tools/llama-bench/llama-bench.cpp:2415-2445`; `L:tools/perplexity/perplexity.cpp:175-294`; `L:src/llama-moe-cache.cpp:585-598`. | Slot verification API, dispatch counters and source-specific tests provide stronger component checks. `S:src/core/expert_cache.cpp:891` and following readback; `S:src/core/expert_source.cpp:5345-5363,5421-5425`; `S:tests/core/expert_cache_slot_guard_test.cpp:1`. | **Yes** joined trace/metrics pipeline with explicit counter definitions and correctness gates. | Trace completeness, slot readback, route/profile split, timeline overlap, uninstrumented throughput and memory accounting. Keep each claim tied to a test. |\n\n## Minimal build sequence\n\n1. **Measure routing first.** Use an out-of-tree cb_eval harness against an ABI-matched existing library. Teacher-force a fixed token corpus and produce complete per-layer/token IDs plus optional weights. Make model/tokenizer identity, output-row settings, microbatch boundaries and resets explicit. This is the cheapest reusable research tool.\n2. **Build a policy simulator.** Replay logical `(layer,expert)` IDs with quantized slice byte sizes. Compare existing llama LRU groups with fixed global/per-layer frequency allocations and decayed-use replacement. Split training and evaluation by prompt/conversation; report expert-hit rate, all-K-resident layer-hit rate, transfer bytes and CPU miss work separately. Llama cache counts unique experts per microbatch; Strata hook counters can count routed occurrences. Recompute a common metric before comparing percentages.\n3. **Use existing cache as a residency baseline.** Sweep eligible host weights, cache bytes and microbatch sizes, including 1, 8, 32 and 33 tokens and the capacity guard. Recreate context for cold-cache trials. Capture actual assignment and cache aggregates; a nominal CPU-placement flag is not execution proof.\n4. **Implement the missing seam in a prototype.** Route once, publish current activation/IDs, take a stable residency snapshot, launch resident expert GPU work, run CPU miss jobs, copy miss outputs and combine on the correct stream. Start with explicit synchronization as a correctness baseline before optimizing notification/capture.\n5. **Gate optimization on numerical proof.** Compare each expert contribution and per-layer residual to a reference at identical input tokens; add all-hit, all-miss, mixed-hit, repeated-ID-across-tokens, changing-layer, eviction-in-flight and reset cases. GPU/CPU activation quantization and current-layer buffer reuse need dedicated fixtures.\n6. **Measure overlap and adaptivity independently.** Keep numerical inputs fixed for overlap-on/off; use CPU+GPU timelines. Only then add profile preload, asynchronous replacements with publication-after-copy, and eventually KV/expert memory lending. Report uninstrumented wall time separately from callback traces.\n\nThis sequence follows D003's methodology emphasis: build tools that can falsify a mechanism claim, then build the mechanism. Source comments describing historical measured numbers are evidence of test intent, not measurements reproduced by this assignment.\n\n## Open questions\n\n- What existing image/library/model can the tracing harness use, and does its revision match these APIs? No image was supplied or inspected by this assignment.\n- Which workload and hardware should define the policy and overlap objective? Source alone does not establish that cache fills or CPU misses are faster on the target.\n- Should the first prototype extend llama.cpp's cache or implement an independent executor? Both need the mixed-dispatch seam; only the latter also needs graph/format integration.\n- D003 flags licensing/attribution for later reuse. This report studies mechanisms and copies no engine implementation; review the actual source/model/third-party licenses before selecting reusable components.\nThe GGML scheduler assigns operations based on preallocated buffers, op support, weight location, offload preference, and propagation (`ggml/src/ggml-backend.cpp:1070-1127,1319-1441`). It partitions the graph when backends change or a new incompatible weight input needs a split, then creates copied inputs (`1445-1549`).\n\nAt compute time, user inputs are copied immediately; reuse is protected by backend events or synchronization; callback-controlled host weights are copied after non-weight inputs (`ggml/src/ggml-backend.cpp:1823-1897`). Copy callback registration is at `src/llama-context.cpp:709-711`. Without eval callback the split is submitted asynchronously; with it, requested node boundaries create graph views and synchronization (`ggml/src/ggml-backend.cpp:1899-1935`). Split completion events protect reuse (`1938-1941`).\n\nThis is tensor/op scheduling. It does not partition one layer's selected experts into concurrent CPU misses and GPU hits. Strata's explicit Launch -> CPU pool -> miss H2D -> Combine seam is a separate component (`Strata:include/strata/core/hit_hook.hpp:13-39`; `Strata:src/core/session.cpp:726-748`).\n\n## KV cache, session state, and cold/warm experiments\n\n- Granite uses attention/KV graph input (`src/models/granite.cpp:143,234-236`). KV allocation/state is independent of expert bank storage.\n- `llama_memory_clear(mem,data)` clears the memory module; sequence removal has a separate API (`src/llama-context.cpp:4385-4402`; `include/llama.h:755` and following sequence APIs). KV clear resets cells/heads and zeroes backing buffers only if `data=true` (`src/llama-kv-cache.cpp:391-401`). It does not unload model weights or clear the MoE LRU.\n- Context state write/read serializes architecture and memory module; per-sequence variants serialize memory for that sequence (`src/llama-context.cpp:3608-3667`). Session-file APIs are declared at `include/llama.h:858-924`. There is no expert-cache snapshot in these state methods.\n- To make the **expert cache** cold, recreate the context/cache. A fresh process is stronger for allocator state but still does not guarantee cold OS page cache. A KV reset alone leaves expert cache and its cumulative counters warm. A restored session skips computation for restored tokens, so those router events are absent.\n- Do not assume generated token index equals router input token index: routing processes the token fed to decode, which produces logits for the next token.\n\n## Memory fit and built-in tools\n\n`llama-fit-params` calls the current `common_fit_params` implementation and prints context, GPU-layer, split and override arguments (`tools/fit-params/fit-params.cpp:30-75`). The README uses the older internal `llama_params_fit` name (`tools/fit-params/README.md:3-6`); source is authoritative here.\n\nFit creates a metadata/no-weight-allocation model/context to estimate model/context/compute memory (`common/fit.cpp:30-76`), tries context reduction where allowed (`373-457`), then layer/partial-MoE placement with tensor overrides (`565-627`). It honors constraints such as explicitly set GPU layer counts (`463-478`). Cache no-allocation accounting exists (`src/llama-moe-cache.cpp:387-401,628-638`; included by `src/llama-context.cpp:3710-3713`). This estimates **capacity**, not trace hit rate, CPU/GPU balance, transfer overlap, or optimal cache policy.\n\n| Tool/hook | What it supplies | What it does not supply |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `llama-bench` | Prompt/generation microbenchmarks (`tools/llama-bench/llama-bench.cpp:2168-2227`), repetitions, placement sweeps including `n_cpu_moe` (`354,434,474,1332`), warmup and KV clears (`2387,2415-2445`). | Representative workload routing or residency correctness. Its parser is separate from common CLI; this revision has no `moe_cache_size`/`--moe-cache-mib` handling in that file. Use a harness or extend it for cache sweeps. |\n| `llama-perplexity` | Teacher-forced corpus NLL/perplexity and logits/KL comparison machinery (`tools/perplexity/perplexity.cpp:109-175,175-294,296-430`). | Direct slot correctness or overlap evidence; large prompt batches may follow a different path than decode. |\n| `llama-debug`, eval example | Tensor values via cb_eval (`examples/debug/debug.cpp:231-234`; `examples/eval-callback/eval-callback.cpp:54-58`). | Complete traces by default: printer truncates each dimension at 3 head/tail values (`common/debug.cpp:79-122,186`); filter does not avoid synchronization/copies (`143-186`). |\n| Public `cb_eval` | Selective tensor observation (`include/llama.h:393-394`; `ggml/include/ggml-backend.h:311-318`; attached at `src/llama-context.cpp:1537`). | A public CPU/GPU hit dispatch override, or attaching to an already-running server. |\n| `GGML_SCHED_DEBUG=1/2` | Split/backend/input and node metadata logging (`ggml/src/ggml-backend.cpp:1146-1194,1551-1553,1962-1963`). | Actual routed ID values, per-expert hits, slot bytes or GPU/CPU overlap. Debug log output must be enabled in the application. |\n| MoE cache destructor log | Unique-expert cache hits/misses and transfer totals (`src/llama-moe-cache.cpp:574-598`). | Per-token routing, resident byte correctness, OS page faults, or proof of mixed CPU/GPU compute. |\n\n## Open questions for execution\n\n1. Which immutable runtime image, binaries, shared libraries, headers and model GGUF are actually available? The inspected source revision alone cannot answer ABI/feature availability in an image.\n2. Which Granite variant, quantization, device and microbatch sizes will be tested? These select the graph/kernel/offload path.\n3. How large are callback perturbations and LRU thrashing on the target workload? Measure separately from uninstrumented throughput.\n4. How closely can CPU/GPU expert outputs match with the chosen quantizers? Compare weights, intermediate expert outputs and logits, not just fluent text.\n?? ../.claude/\n?? ./\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
